"(PDF) Universal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/400894826_Universal_priors_solving_empirical_Bayes_via_Bayesian_inference_and_pretraining)\nciteturn31937search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Universal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining  * February 2026 ... Nick Cannella, Anzo Teh, Yanjun Han, Yury Polyanskiy∗ ... Then for every prior G 0 supported\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Universal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining\n\n  * February 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2602.15136\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nNick Cannella\n\nNick Cannella\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nAnzo Teh\n\nAnzo Teh\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nYanjun Han\n\nYanjun Han\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nYury Polyanskiy\n\nYury Polyanskiy\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe theoretically justify the recent empirical finding of [Teh et al., 2025] that a transformer pretrained on synthetically generated data achieves strong performance on empirical Bayes (EB) problems. Join for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nUniversal priors: solving empirical Bayes\n\nvia Bayesian inference and pretraining\n\nNick Cannella, Anzo Teh, Yanjun Han, Yury Polyanskiy∗\n\nFebruary 18, 2026\n\nAbstract\n\nWe theoretically justify the recent empirical ﬁnding of [TJP25] that a transformer pretrained\n\non synthetically generated data achieves strong performance on empirical Bayes (EB) problems.\n\nWe take an indirect approach to this question: rather than analyzing the model architecture\n\nor training dynamics, we ask why a pretrained Bayes estimator, trained under a prespeciﬁed\n\ntraining distribution, can adapt to arbitrary test distributions. Focusing on Poisson EB problems,\n\nwe identify the existence of universal priors such that training under these priors yields a near-\n\noptimal regret bound of e\n\nO(1\n\nn)uniformly over all test distributions. Our analysis leverages the\n\nclassical phenomenon of posterior contraction in Bayesian statistics, showing that the pretrained\n\ntransformer adapts to unknown test distributions precisely through posterior contraction. This\n\nperspective also explains the phenomenon of length generalization, in which the test sequence\n\nlength exceeds the training length, as the model performs Bayesian inference using a generalized\n\nposterior.\n\nContents\n\n1 Introduction 2\n\n1.1 Main result I: Universal priors ............................... 5\n\n1.2 Main result II: Length generalization ........................... 6\n\n1.3 Related work ........................................ 8\n\n2 Analysis via posterior contraction 9\n\n2.1 Preliminaries ........................................ 9\n\n2.2 Posterior contraction .................................... 10\n\n2.3 Posterior contraction for the α-posterior ......................... 11\n\n3 Discussions 11\n\n3.1 Finite number of batches .................................. 11\n\n3.2 Subexponential priors ................................... 12\n\n3.3 Gaussian empirical Bayes problem ............................ 12\n\n3.4 Function estimation .................................... 13\n\n∗N.C. is with the Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences, NYU, New York City, NY, email: nvc9912@nyu.\n\nedu; Y.H. is with the Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences and Center for Data Science, NYU, New York\n\nCity, NY, email: yanjunhan@nyu.edu; A.T. and Y.P. are with Department of EECS, MIT, Cambridge, MA, email:\n\nanzoteh@mit.edu and yp@mit.edu.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGMAIL: Generative Modality Alignment for generated Image Learning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15368)\nciteturn31937academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: GMAIL: Generative Modality Alignment for generated Image Learning ... Date: Tue Feb 17 05:40:25 2026\nTitle: GMAIL: Generative Modality Alignment for generated Image Learning\nAuthors: Shentong Mo, Sukmin Yun\nDate: Tue Feb 17 05:40:25 2026\n\nGenerative models have made it possible to synthesize highly realistic images, potentially providing an abundant data source for training machine learning models. Despite the advantages of these synthesizable data sources, the indiscriminate use of generated images as real images for training can even cause mode collapse due to modality discrepancies between real and synthetic domains. In this paper, we propose a novel framework for discriminative use of generated images, coined GMAIL, that explicitly treats generated images as a separate modality from real images. Instead of indiscriminately replacing real images with generated ones in the pixel space, our approach bridges the two distinct modalities in the same latent space through a multi-modal learning approach. To be specific, we first fine-tune a model exclusively on generated images using a cross-modality alignment loss and then employ this aligned model to further train various vision-language models with generated images. By aligning the two modalities, our approach effectively leverages the benefits of recent advances in generative models, thereby boosting the effectiveness of generated image learning across a range of vision-language tasks. Our framework can be easily incorporated with various vision-language models, and we demonstrate its efficacy throughout extensive experiments. For example, our framework significantly improves performance on image captioning, zero-shot image retrieval, zero-shot image classification, and long caption retrieval tasks. It also shows positive generated data scaling trends and notable enhancements in the captioning performance of the large multimodal model, LLaVA.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVideoSketcher: Video Models Prior Enable Versatile Sequential Sketch Generation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15819)\nciteturn31937academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: VideoSketcher: Video Models Prior Enable Versatile Sequential Sketch GenerationAuthors: Hui Ren, Yuval Alaluf, Omer Bar Tal, Alexander Schwing, Antonio Torralba, Yael VinkerDate: Tue Feb 17 18:55:03 2026\nTitle: VideoSketcher: Video Models Prior Enable Versatile Sequential Sketch Generation\nAuthors: Hui Ren, Yuval Alaluf, Omer Bar Tal, Alexander Schwing, Antonio Torralba, Yael Vinker\nDate: Tue Feb 17 18:55:03 2026\n\nSketching is inherently a sequential process, in which strokes are drawn in a meaningful order to explore and refine ideas. However, most generative models treat sketches as static images, overlooking the temporal structure that underlies creative drawing. We present a data-efficient approach for sequential sketch generation that adapts pretrained text-to-video diffusion models to generate sketching processes. Our key insight is that large language models and video diffusion models offer complementary strengths for this task: LLMs provide semantic planning and stroke ordering, while video diffusion models serve as strong renderers that produce high-quality, temporally coherent visuals. We leverage this by representing sketches as short videos in which strokes are progressively drawn on a blank canvas, guided by text-specified ordering instructions. We introduce a two-stage fine-tuning strategy that decouples the learning of stroke ordering from the learning of sketch appearance. Stroke ordering is learned using synthetic shape compositions with controlled temporal structure, while visual appearance is distilled from as few as seven manually authored sketching processes that capture both global drawing order and the continuous formation of individual strokes. Despite the extremely limited amount of human-drawn sketch data, our method generates high-quality sequential sketches that closely follow text-specified orderings while exhibiting rich visual detail. We further demonstrate the flexibility of our approach through extensions such as brush style conditioning and autoregressive sketch generation, enabling additional controllability and interactive, collaborative drawing.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n汎用事前分布：ベイズ推論と事前学習による経験的ベイズの解決 | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ja/overview/2602.15136v1)\nciteturn31937search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Universal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining ... February 16, 2026Nick Cannella, Anzo Teh, Yanjun Han, Yury Polyanskiy\n\n# Universal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining\n\n汎用事前分布：ベイズ推論と事前学習による経験的ベイズの解決\n\nFebruary 16, 2026\n\nNick Cannella, Anzo Teh, Yanjun Han, Yury Polyanskiy\n\n### 要約\n\n厳密な理論的枠組みが、特定の「ユニバーサル事前分布」で事前学習されたトランスフォーマーが、経験ベイズ問題において準最適に近い頻度主義的後悔を達成する方法を厳密に説明している。NYUとMITの共同研究であるこの研究は、\\u03b1-事後推論を通じて長さの一般化のメカニズムを解明し、深層学習モデルが複雑な推論タスクで適応的な性能を発揮することに統計的な正当性を提供している。\n\n厳密な理論的枠組みが、特定の「ユニバーサル事前分布」で事前学習されたトランスフォーマーが、経験ベイズ問題において準最適に近い頻度主義的後悔を達成する方法を厳密に説明している。--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGMAIL: Generative Modality Alignment for generated Image Learning | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202602221270551122)\nciteturn31937search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # GMAIL: Generative Modality Alignment for generated Image Learning ... Publication year： Feb. 17, 2026  update date on preprint serve